<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Siricius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 399</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Siricius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siricius was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from 384 to 399. His letter of 385 to Bishop Himerius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a>, answering questions on baptism, penance and clerical discipline, is the oldest surviving papal decretal, a ruling with the force of law. His rulings also required the higher clergy to live in continence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-siricius.jpg" alt="Pope Siricius" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Master of the Altarpiece of Santa Auta · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pormenor_do_Ret%C3%A1bulo_de_Santa_Auta_(Papa_Cir%C3%ADaco_Aben%C3%A7oa_Santa_Auta_e_o_Pr%C3%ADncipe_Conan),_Museu_Nacional_de_Arte_Antiga.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
